I think this function is not actually doing what the javadoc advertises. I'd suggest to change it to this:
```suggestion
        if (format.height != Format.NO_VALUE) {
            final StringBuilder resolutionStringBuilder = new StringBuilder();
            resolutionStringBuilder.append(format.height);
            resolutionStringBuilder.append("p");

            if (format.frameRate != Format.NO_VALUE) {
                final int frameRate = Math.round(format.frameRate);
                if (frameRate > 30) {
                    resolutionStringBuilder.append(frameRate);
                }
            }

            return resolutionStringBuilder.toString();

        } else if (format.bitrate != Format.NO_VALUE) {
            // If height is unknown, use the bitrate as a last resort
            return format.bitrate + "bps";

        } else {
            // We don't have information to distinguish format's quality,
            // return an unknown quality string as a last resort
            return context.getString(R.string.unknown_quality);
        }
```

Also, small note: it's better not to use String.valueOf() if you are then passing that data to a builder. The builder already takes care of optimizing the conversion of the number to string lazily. ;-)
